        <p>Yes. At Mohs 8, Blue Topaz is durable for daily wear, but it has perfect cleavage and can be damaged by strong impact. A protective setting is advisable for rings worn during physical activity.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'tiger-eye' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What gives Tiger's Eye its chatoyancy?</strong>
        <p>Tiger's Eye is formed from quartz that has pseudomorphically replaced crocidolite fibres, leaving parallel silky fibres within the stone that create its distinctive moving band of reflected light when polished as a cabochon.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Tiger's Eye suitable for astrological use?</strong>
        <p>Yes. Tiger's Eye is associated with Sun and Mars energy and is commonly used for building confidence, mental focus, and protection — particularly as an accessible semi-precious stone for these qualities.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Is Tiger's Eye dyed?</strong>
        <p>Natural Tiger's Eye is golden-brown to honey-brown in colour. Blue Hawks Eye is a natural variety. Red Tiger's Eye is typically heat-treated natural Tiger's Eye. Always confirm natural colour through certification for quality purchases.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'purple-sapphire' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What is Purple Sapphire astrologically?</strong>
        <p>Purple Sapphire carries a blended Venus-Saturn energy, making it associated with spirituality, wisdom, and deep inner knowing. It sits between Blue and Pink in the sapphire spectrum.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Is Purple Sapphire as powerful as Blue Sapphire?</strong>
        <p>Not in the same direct way. Purple Sapphire carries a more refined, spiritual quality compared to Blue Sapphire's direct Saturn intensity. Consultation is still required before wearing either.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Are Purple Sapphires treated?</strong>
        <p>Like all sapphires, they may be heat-treated or untreated. Untreated specimens are rarer and more valuable. Always confirm treatment status through independent certification before purchasing.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'padparadscha' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What makes a genuine Padparadscha?</strong>
        <p>Padparadscha must display a specific balance of pink and orange colour — neither purely pink (that would be a pink sapphire) nor purely orange. The sunset hue must be present in both components simultaneously. This precise colour is extremely rare.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>Are Padparadscha sapphires treated?</strong>
        <p>Many are heat-treated or colour-enhanced. Untreated Padparadscha sapphires are extraordinarily rare and command significant premiums. Always insist on independent certification that explicitly states treatment status.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Which origin produces the best Padparadscha?</strong>
        <p>Sri Lanka (Ceylon) is the traditional and most highly regarded origin for Padparadscha. Madagascar and Tanzania also produce fine specimens. Origin should always be independently verified for premium pieces.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'star-sapphire' -%}
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What creates the star in Star Sapphire?</strong>
        <p>The six-rayed star is caused by asterism — light reflecting off three sets of parallel needle-like rutile inclusions (silk) arranged at 60-degree angles within the stone. The star is only visible when polished as a cabochon under direct light.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px; padding-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">
        <strong>What makes a quality Star Sapphire?</strong>
        <p>A sharp, well-centred star with all six rays clearly defined, a rich body colour, good transparency in the body, and natural origin are the hallmarks of a premium Star Sapphire. A weak, off-centre, or broken star significantly reduces value.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Is Star Sapphire suitable for Saturn remedies?</strong>
        <p>Yes. Star Sapphire shares Saturn's qualities and carries an additional symbolism of guidance and destiny. Consultation confirming Saturn's favourability in the chart is essential before wearing.</p>
      </div>
  {%- endcase -%}
